{#
  One row of the receiving bay form (#792, mockup/receive-flow-7's "What arrived" table).

  Shared by the order's own lines and by the spare rows *Add more lines* produces, so the two cannot
  drift into offering different boxes — which is exactly what happened to this screen the last time
  it had two row shapes. Also shared between the with-PO and without-PO screens: `order` (read from
  the parent context — this partial is never included with `only`) decides whether the PO/Outstanding
  columns exist at all, and everything else renders identically either way.

  ## The boxes rendered are the boxes that MEAN something (item 67)

  `needs` is a CaptureRequirement, built from the same ProductReceivingRule that ReceivingService
  refuses on — whose batch, expiry and serial answers derive from the product's TRACKING POLICY, so
  there is one description of what this product needs and not two. A product that tracks nothing
  gets no batch box and no serial box, because the walkthrough found both typed against exactly such
  a product and stored. A product that carries a serial gets the serial block and NOT a batch box.
  What the row needs is stated in words above the boxes, on every row including the ones that need
  nothing — silence was being read as "nobody has told me".

  ## Lot Skip and Expiry Skip are independent (#792)

  Each identity dimension carries its OWN real checkbox next to its own box — `f-none` beside the
  lot code (`[unidentified]`, unchanged since item 67/#573) and `f-noexp` beside the expiry
  (`[expiry_unidentified]`, new) — styled as a toggle button by `.skip-toggle` in app.css but a plain
  checkbox underneath, so it posts and works exactly the same with scripting off. A receiver who has
  the batch code but genuinely has no expiry for it ticks Expiry Skip alone; ticking Lot Skip does
  not touch the expiry box, and the other way round. ReceivingService::assertLineIsBookable() reads
  the two independently — see its `$declared` and `$expiryDeclared`.

  Serial-tracked rows carry no Skip at all: "Serial-tracked rows need one serial per unit" is a rule
  this screen does not offer an escape from, on this table. (`unidentified` can still excuse a blank
  serial when posted directly — #573's declaration was never restricted to lot-tracked products in
  ReceivingService — but nothing on this table invites that for a serial row, matching the mockup.)

  ## The short-dated reason box (item 68)

  Rendered wherever an expiry box is, because that is the only place a date can be short. It is
  blank on an ordinary delivery and stays blank: a reason typed on a line that turns out not to be
  short records nothing. When the date IS inside the minimum shelf life, booking in is refused until
  it is filled — and everything typed comes back on the re-render, so the reason is added to the
  form that was refused rather than retyped into a fresh one.

  ## Why nothing here carries `required`

  A row on this form is optional: every line of a purchase order appears whether or not it turned
  up, and a receiver skips one by leaving it blank. `required` cannot say "required only if this row
  is being received" without JavaScript, and this app's baseline is that it works without any. So
  the enforcement is ReceivingService's, where it can see the quantity beside the box, and every
  refusal names the line and says what is missing.

  Parameters
  ----------
  index            required  the row's position in `lines[]`
  orderLine        required  the PurchaseOrderLine this row is fixed to, or null for a spare row
  needs            required  the CaptureRequirement for this row's product, or null
  defaultQuantity  optional  pre-filled quantity — the order line's outstanding balance
  defaultUnitCost  optional  pre-filled unit cost — the order line's cost
#}
{% set idx = 'lines[' ~ index ~ ']' %}
{% set row = draft[index] ?? {} %}
<tr>
    <td data-label="Item">
        {% if orderLine %}
            {{ orderLine.name }}
            <input type="hidden" name="{{ idx }}[purchase_order_line_id]" value="{{ orderLine.id }}">
        {% else %}
            {# `selected` is the ENTITY the controller resolved for this row, never `row.product_id`.
               `draft` is a redisplay and holds the strings that were posted, while the shared
               partial evaluates `selected.id` — so handing it the string turned every re-render of a
               spare row with a product chosen into a 500. That fired on *Add more lines* and on ANY
               refusal, which is precisely when this re-render exists to give a receiver back the
               block of serials they pasted. #}
            {{ include('@Procurement/_product_field.html.twig', {
                field: idx ~ '[product_id]',
                products: productOptions,
                remote: productsRemote,
                selected: draftProducts[index]|default(null),
                vendorId: null,
                label: 'Product for line ' ~ (index + 1),
            }) }}
        {% endif %}

        {% if needs %}<br><small>{{ needs.describe }}</small>{% endif %}
    </td>
    {% if order %}
        <td data-label="PO" style="text-align:right;">{{ orderLine ? orderLine.quantityOrdered|qty : '—' }}</td>
        <td data-label="O/S" style="text-align:right;">{{ orderLine ? orderLine.quantityOutstanding|qty : '—' }}</td>
    {% endif %}
    <td class="qty-td" data-label="{{ order ? 'Arriving now' : 'Quantity arriving' }}">
        <input type="text" name="{{ idx }}[quantity]" value="{{ row.quantity|default(defaultQuantity|default('')) }}"
               inputmode="decimal" aria-label="Quantity arriving">
        {# Recorded and never applied to the product's own cost (see GoodsReceiptLine's own
           docblock) — carried through as the order line's cost, or whatever was typed on a
           previous pass of this same row, without its own column on this table. #}
        <input type="hidden" name="{{ idx }}[unit_cost]" value="{{ row.unit_cost|default(defaultUnitCost|default('')) }}">
    </td>
    <td class="id-c" data-label="Lot">
        {% if needs and needs.capturesLot %}
            <div class="skip-row">
                <input type="text" name="{{ idx }}[lot_code]" maxlength="64" value="{{ row.lot_code|default('') }}"
                       placeholder="Lot code" aria-label="Lot code">
                {% if needs.unidentifiedMayBeDeclared %}
                    <label class="skip-toggle" title="Arrived with no lot: goes on the tracking worklist">
                        <input type="checkbox" name="{{ idx }}[unidentified]" value="1"
                               {{ row.unidentified|default('') ? 'checked' : '' }} aria-label="Arrived with no lot">
                        <span>Skip</span>
                    </label>
                {% endif %}
            </div>
        {% else %}
            <small>—</small>
        {% endif %}
    </td>
    <td class="id-c" data-label="Expiry">
        {% if needs and needs.capturesExpiry %}
            <div class="skip-row">
                <input type="date" name="{{ idx }}[expiry]" value="{{ row.expiry|default('') }}" aria-label="Expiry">
                <label class="skip-toggle" title="No expiry date on the goods">
                    <input type="checkbox" name="{{ idx }}[expiry_unidentified]" value="1"
                           {{ row.expiry_unidentified|default('') ? 'checked' : '' }} aria-label="Arrived with no expiry date">
                    <span>Skip</span>
                </label>
            </div>
            {{ include('@Procurement/_short_dated_reason.html.twig', {idx: idx, index: index, row: row}) }}
        {% else %}
            <small>—</small>
        {% endif %}
    </td>
    <td class="id-c" data-label="Serial">
        {% if needs and needs.capturesSerial %}
            {# Bulk entry, and the reason it is a textarea rather than a repeated input is in
               SerialList's docblock: a vendor's manifest is a COLUMN, and a wedge scanner pointed at
               a textarea types the serial and inserts a newline instead of submitting — so seventy
               units can be scanned into one box, with no JavaScript. #}
            <label class="sr-only" for="{{ idx }}-serials">Serials for line {{ index + 1 }}, one per line</label>
            <textarea id="{{ idx }}-serials" name="{{ idx }}[serials]" rows="3" cols="16"
                      placeholder="One per line">{{ row.serials|default('') }}</textarea>
            <br><small>One serial per unit. Up to {{ serialLimit }}. Leave the quantity blank and they count themselves.</small>
        {% else %}
            <small>—</small>
        {% endif %}
    </td>
    <td data-label="Bin">
        <select name="{{ idx }}[location_id]" aria-label="Bin">
            <option value="">—</option>
            {% for bin in bins %}
                <option value="{{ bin.id }}" {{ row.location_id|default('') == bin.id ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ bin.code }}{{ order ? '' : ' @ ' ~ bin.warehouse.name }}</option>
            {% endfor %}
        </select>
        {% if needs and needs.location %}<br><small>Required — cannot be skipped</small>{% endif %}
    </td>
</tr>
